      "text": "DAFT: Research Procedure—Results So Far—Future DAFT Tests—Additional Information\nResearch Procedure During the ISS tests conducted in 2005,\nastronauts performed several steps:\n����\n1. Turned on the P-Trak® and the DustTrak®\n2. Attached High Efficiency Particulate Air (HEPA) filters to the inlets\n������\nto “zero” the particle counters (since the HEPA filter removes all �������� ���������\n������ ������\nairborne particles, if the particle counters don’t read “0” something\n�����\nis amiss)\n����� ��������\n3. Connected the devices to a common sampling hose ������\n4. Recorded the ISS environment for several minutes\n5. Reconnected the HEPA filters to “zero” the particle counters\n���������\n6. Turned off the P-Trak® and the DustTrak® ��������\nThe tests were conducted in the US laboratory module Destiny in front\nof EXPRESS Rack 4; in the modules’s aft end and port side; and in ��������� �����������\n�������� ������������\nNode 1. (The EXPRESS Rack is a standardized payload rack system that\ntransports, stores, and supports experiments aboard the ISS; Node 1 is a\n�����\nhub to which various modules are attached.) Three of the tests sampled\n����������\nundisturbed cabin air; the other test validated the instruments at high ���������������\nparticulate levels by having an astronaut create airborne debris at the P-\nAs shown in this P-Trak® schematic, particle-\nTrak® and DustTrak® inlets by separating pieces of Velcro®.\nladen air passes through a heated chamber\ncontaining vaporized alcohol and then into a\nResults So Far The three tests that sampled the undisturbed cooled chamber where the alcohol droplets\nform around the particles. The particles are\nenvironment in the ISS showed very low levels of airborne\ncounted as they pass through a laser beam.\nparticulates, averaging fewer than 0.005 mg/m3 from the DustTrak®\nand fewer than 15 particles/cm3 from the P-Trak® (these are typical\nP-Trak® DustTrak®\nreadings at the baseline). These numbers are dramatically lower than\nSize 10.75 x 5.5 x 5.5 8.7 x 5.9 x 3.4\nthe values recorded in the Space Shuttle in an earlier experiment (~ (inches)\n.050 mg/m3). Lower levels are to be expected because the ISS US Weight 3.8 3.3\n(lbs)\nLab has HEPA filtration for the cabin air as compared to merely a fine\nParticle 0.02 to > 1.0 0.10 to 10\nscreen on the shuttle air handler. Furthermore, the typical shuttle crew size range\nof seven astronauts generates more airborne particulate than an ISS (mm)\ncrew of two astronauts.\nThe P-Trak® and the DustTrak® compared.\nFuture DAFT Tests The next series of tests will take place once\nthe remaining DAFT equipment is delivered to the ISS. These will be\nperformed with Arizona Road Dust (ARD)—a standard aerosal test\nmaterial of known particle size and distribution—and nitrogen aerosol\ninside a 15-liter Mylar® bag. Testing a known particulate will allow\nscientists to establish definitively how well the P-Trak® works in\nmicrogravity.\nAdditional Information More experiment details—including\nperiodic updates and photos—can be found on the DAFT Web site:\nhttp://microgravity.grc.nasa.gov/combustion/daft/daft_index.htm. The DustTrak®—shown here with its battery\npack—is insensitive to gravitational forces.\nIt is used to determine the accuracy of the\nmodified P-Trak®.",
